```java
public static String longestCommonSubstring(String s1, String s2) {
    int m = s1.length();
    int n = s2.length();
    int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    String lcs = "";

    int[][] dp = new int[m+1][n+1];

    for(int i=1; i<=m; i++) {
        for(int j=1; j<=n; j++) {
            if(s1.charAt(i-1) == s2.charAt(j-1)) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;
                if(max < dp[i][j]) {
                    max = dp[i][j];
                    lcs = s1.substring(i-max, i);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return lcs;
}
```

The `longestCommonSubstring` method takes two input strings `s1` and `s2` and returns the longest common substring between them. It uses a 2D dynamic programming table `dp` to store the lengths of the common substrings at each index.

The outer `for` loop iterates over the characters of `s1`, and the inner `for` loop iterates over the characters of `s2`. If the characters at the current indices are equal, we increment the length of the common substring by 1 (`dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1`). 

If the length of the current common substring is greater than the previous maximum, we update the `max` and store the substring starting from index `i-max` to index `i` in the variable `lcs`. Finally, we return `lcs`, which represents the longest common substring.

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2) because it iterates over each character of both input strings once in nested loops.

