For any unimodal function $f(x)$ on $[0,1]$, the following explores methods to shorten the length of its peak interval.  
(I) Prove that: For any $x_1, x_2 \in (0,1)$, $x_1 < x_2$, if $f(x_1) \geq f(x_2)$, then $(0,x_2)$ is a peak interval; if $f(x_1) \leq f(x_2)$, then $(x_1,1)$ is a peak interval;  
(II) For a given $r$ ($0<r<0.5$), prove that there exist $x_1, x_2 \in (0,1)$, satisfying $x_2 - x_1 \geq 2r$, such that the length of the peak interval determined by (I) is no greater than $0.5+r$;  
